<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="activity-27b" top="0.5in" left="0.5in" right="0.5in" bottom="0.5in">
  <title>Lecture Worksheet - Relativistic Rocket</title>

  <!-- Optional introduction -->
  <introduction>
    <p>
      This is the introduction to the activity.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <!-- Exercises start here. -->
  <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The picture below shows a relativistic rocket!
        The difference from a non-relativistic rocket is that the ''exhaust
        speed'' <m>
          \beta_{r}
      </m> can be relativistic.
        In particular, we could imagine a rocket driven by matter-antimatter
        annihilation, shooting photons out of the back, for which
        <m>
          \beta_{r}=1
      </m>.
      </p>

      <image source="images\activities\lect27\rocketsketch.png"/>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              In Newtonian mechanics, we can say that
              <m>u_{x}=V+dV-\beta_{r}</m> (the fragments move with speed
              <m>\beta_{r}</m> to the left of the rocket) and <m>dm=-dM</m> (the
              mass flying out of the rocket = the mass lost by the rocket,
              noting that <m>dM &lt; 0</m> here).
              Is either of these statements true in relativity?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Since <m>\beta_{r}</m> is defined in the rest frame of the rocket,
              redraw the before-and-after picture in the instantaneous rest
              frame of the rocket.
              Be careful: is <m>dV'</m>, the velocity increment in this frame,
              the same as <m>dV</m>?
              No need to calculate anything (yet), just draw a picture.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              In this type of problem, we only keep ''first order''
              infinitesimals like <m>dV',dm,dM</m>.
              Any products like <m>(dV')^{2}, dmdV</m>, would be set to zero.
              What is the <m>\gamma</m> of the rocket in this approximation?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Using conservation of energy and conservation of momentum, solve
              for <m>dV'</m> in terms of <m>dM</m>.
              Note that there are three unknowns here: <m>dM, dm, dV'</m>, so
              you need to eliminate <m>dm</m> first.
              Notation: call <m>\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{1-\beta_{r}^{2}}}=\gamma_{r}</m>
              so we can keep up with what's on the board.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Use the Einstein velocity addition formula to find what
              <m>V+dV</m> is in the rocket frame.
              Meaning: after shooting out mass <m>dm</m>, the rocket moves with
              velocity <m>dV'</m> relative to the previous rest frame, which had
              velocity <m>+V</m> relative to the ground.
              Use the Taylor expansion <m>\dfrac{1}{1+x}\simeq 1-x</m> for
              <m>x \ll 1</m>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p>
        Integrating this rocket equation isn't as easy as the regular rocket
        equation, so I'll just give you the answer, which is not totally
        simplified but good enough if you wanted to solve a particular problem.
      <md>
        \frac{\xi_{f}}{\xi_{i}}=\left(\frac{M_{i}}{M_{f}}\right)^{\beta},\,\, \text{where}\,\, \xi=\sqrt{\dfrac{1+v}{1-v}}.
      </md>
      </p>


      <p>
        Challenge for you, if you are bored over break: show that if both
        <m>\beta \ll 1</m> and <m>v \ll 1</m>, some Taylor expansion work gets
        you back to the non-relativistic rocket equation:
        <m>\Delta v = \beta \ln\left(\dfrac{M_i}{M_f}\right)</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>


</worksheet>